-- A second BPMN fixture, chosen to cover the constructs `offering.l4` does not:
--
--   * a deadline that is a NAME rather than a number, which no amount of
--     parsing turns into an ISO 8601 duration (so: a conditional boundary
--     event carrying the text verbatim, and a loud fidelity finding);
--   * a RAND whose branches carry deadlines, which is the ordinary case and
--     draws a split with NO join. Each permission's WITHIN becomes a lapse
--     timer, giving that branch a second and mutually exclusive way to reach
--     the join point, and a converging parallel gateway would wait for both.
--     This file used to advertise itself as the join exhibit and was wrong
--     about it: see `consultation.l4`, which is what a joinable RAND has to
--     look like, and `P-NOJOIN` in the fidelity report here for why this one
--     is not;
--   * a ROR, which must come out as an exclusive gateway, not a parallel one.
--
-- Fixture for `jl4/tests/BpmnExport.hs`.

DECLARE Party IS ONE OF Tenant, Landlord

DECLARE Step IS ONE OF
  `give vacant possession`
  `inspect the premises`
  `photograph the premises`
  `retain the deposit`
  `apply to the tribunal`

`grace period` MEANS 14

`the handover` MEANS
  PARTY Tenant
  MUST `give vacant possession`
  WITHIN `grace period`
  HENCE
        ((PARTY Landlord MAY `inspect the premises` WITHIN 7)
    RAND (PARTY Landlord MAY `photograph the premises` WITHIN 7))
  LEST
        ((PARTY Landlord MAY `retain the deposit` WITHIN 30)
     ROR (PARTY Landlord MAY `apply to the tribunal` WITHIN 30))
